{"id":"pattern-example-001","type":"pattern","fact":"Example: Use mock factories for all test data instead of manual mock objects with 'as Type' assertions.","recommendation":"Create centralized mock factory functions (createMockUser, createMockOrg, etc.) and import them in tests.","confidence":"high","provenance":[{"source":"documentation","reference":"Testing guide","date":"2026-01-09"}],"tags":["testing","mocks","factories"],"affectedFiles":["**/*.test.ts"],"affectedServices":[],"createdAt":"2026-01-09T00:00:00Z","updatedAt":"2026-01-09T00:00:00Z","usageCount":0,"helpfulCount":0,"outdatedReports":0}
{"id":"pattern-iqr-001","type":"pattern","fact":"For Type 7 IQR Q1 interpolation when frac1>0 (i.e. (n-1)%4 != 0, ~75% of n values): selecting to lo1+1 then max-scanning buf[0..lo1] (O(0.25n)) is faster than selecting to lo1 then min-scanning buf[lo1+1..n-1] (O(0.75n)). After pdqselect(buf, buf+lo1+1, buf+n), buf[lo1+1] is correct and buf[0..lo1] holds the lo1+1 smallest values; max of those is the lo1-th order statistic. Q3 selection then starts at lo1+2 (not lo1+1). Gate: n=100 -4.3%, n=1000 -3.3%.","recommendation":"Use this symmetric pattern for any Type 7 quantile interpolation where frac>0. The max-scan loop must be hand-written (not std::max_element) to enable VMAXPD auto-vectorization.","confidence":"high","provenance":[{"source":"agent","reference":"iqr-opt OPT-I3","date":"2026-03-22"}],"tags":["iqr","quantile","pdqselect","performance","simd"],"affectedFiles":["src/iqr.cpp","src/estimators_internal.h"],"createdAt":"2026-03-22T00:00:00Z","updatedAt":"2026-03-22T00:00:00Z","usageCount":0,"helpfulCount":0,"outdatedReports":0}
{"id":"pattern-sn-001","type":"pattern","fact":"Workspace-accepting overloads for estimators with large-n heap paths: add 'double estimator(const T* x, size_t n, T* workspace)' that uses the workspace buffer for the heap allocation when n > stack_threshold and delegates to the existing stack path otherwise. In ensemble_one_replicate, pass work1 (free before the sn call at line 145, first written by rob_scale_compute at line ~162) as the workspace. Pattern eliminates 1000+ new[]/delete[] cycles per scale_robust_ci call at large n.","recommendation":"Apply this pattern to any estimator called in the bootstrap loop whose large-n path heap-allocates. Check work1 / work2 lifetime at the call site before passing — use ensemble.cpp line comments as reference. The workspace overload must silently ignore workspace when n <= stack_threshold (don't force the caller to check thresholds).","confidence":"high","provenance":[{"source":"agent","reference":"sn-opt OPT-S7 workspace reuse","date":"2026-03-22"}],"tags":["performance","bootstrap","ensemble","heap-allocation","rcpp","sn"],"affectedFiles":["src/sn_estimator.cpp","src/estimators_internal.h","src/ensemble.cpp"],"affectedServices":[],"createdAt":"2026-03-22T00:00:00Z","updatedAt":"2026-03-22T00:00:00Z","usageCount":0,"helpfulCount":0,"outdatedReports":0}
{"id":"pattern-debug-001","type":"pattern","fact":"For suspected C++ infinite loops with a 'state reuse' hypothesis: test the hypothesis with a targeted compile-flag or constant change before spending multiple rounds on static analysis. Setting QN_WS_THRESHOLD=0 to force nullptr, recompiling, and running the hang-triggering call took 5 minutes and immediately falsified the workspace-reuse hypothesis — resolving what 3+ static analysis rounds could not.","recommendation":"When an infinite loop has multiple plausible causes, create a minimal binary probe (one constant change, one recompile, one test) for each hypothesis in order of cheapness. Static analysis alone is unreliable for C++ aliasing/initialization timing issues. Empirical falsification is faster and more conclusive.","confidence":"high","provenance":[{"source":"agent","reference":"qn-opt WU-Q8 debugging: workspace hypothesis falsified by QN_WS_THRESHOLD=0 test","date":"2026-03-23"}],"tags":["debugging","infinite-loop","hypothesis-testing","c++","methodology"],"affectedFiles":["src/ensemble.cpp","src/qn_estimator.cpp"],"affectedServices":[],"createdAt":"2026-03-23T00:00:00Z","updatedAt":"2026-03-23T00:00:00Z","usageCount":0,"helpfulCount":0,"outdatedReports":0}
{"id":"pattern-rl-001","type":"pattern","fact":"NR sum permutation invariance enables warm-cache optimization: Σtanh((x_i-t)*h) depends only on the multiset of x, not element ordering. After median_select(buf) scrambles buf into a permuted copy of xp, buf and xp produce identical NR sums. Pass buf (warm in L1/L2 after median+MAD computation) instead of cold xp to the NR loop to exploit cache locality without any correctness risk.","recommendation":"When the NR kernel reads xp without writing to it, and buf is a permuted copy of xp already in cache, pass buf. Verify invariance holds (sum, not ordered product) before applying.","confidence":"high","provenance":["WU-RL2-RL3 OPT-RL3 warm buf pass"],"tags":["cpp","performance","cache","nr","robloc"],"affectedFiles":["src/rob_loc.cpp"],"affectedServices":[],"createdAt":"2026-03-24","updatedAt":"2026-03-24","usageCount":0,"helpfulCount":0,"outdatedReports":0}
